<h2>A death, and three revisions</h2>
<p>The deceased married in 2016 and died in July 2018 after falling from the terrace of the matrimonial home. Her family alleged repeated dowry-related monetary demands, physical violence, and pressure concerning the sale of a flat. The accused denied the allegations, pointed out that the couple had been living apart for part of June and July 2018, and relied on a viscera report showing alcohol intoxication to suggest the fall may have been accidental. The trial court&rsquo;s order of 18 December 2019 split the difference: it discharged the husband under Section 304B and his parents under Sections 304B and 498A, but directed that the husband face charges under Sections 498A and 306 IPC, which were framed in February 2020. The complainant and the State challenged the discharges; the husband challenged his charges. All three revisions were heard together.</p>

<h2>The charge-stage standard, applied strictly</h2>
<p>The governing test under Sections 227 and 228 CrPC asks whether the material discloses grave suspicion of the particular offence. The Court&rsquo;s formulation is the heart of the judgment: the sifting of material at the charge stage &ldquo;does not in any case dispense with the requirement of a factual foundation for the offence alleged.&rdquo; Grave suspicion, in other words, must attach to the ingredients of the specific offence — not to the tragedy in general.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Section 304B: discharges affirmed</h3><p>No dowry nexus with a proximate link to the death was shown against the husband; against the in-laws, the allegations were collective and lacked particulars. Without the foundational ingredients, the Section 113B presumption never engages.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Section 306: charge set aside</h3><p>No positive act of instigation or intentional aid appeared; the trial court had wrongly read the final message in isolation; and the Section 113A presumption does not automatically establish abetment of suicide.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Section 498A: charge set aside</h3><p>Explanation (b) — harassment for an unlawful demand — was not made out, and &ldquo;the material does not raise grave suspicion of the particular offence contemplated by Explanation (a).&rdquo;</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The operative result</h2>
<p>The complainant&rsquo;s revision (CRL.REV.P. 29/2020) and the State&rsquo;s revision (CRL.REV.P. 297/2022) were dismissed; the husband&rsquo;s revision (CRL.REV.P. 170/2021) was allowed. The charges framed on 5 February 2020 under Sections 498A and 306 IPC were set aside and the husband stands discharged; the trial court&rsquo;s discharge orders of 18 December 2019 were upheld in full, pending applications disposed of and interim orders vacated.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Why the judgment matters:</strong> matrimonial-death cases generate powerful moral pressure to send every accusation to trial, and the twin presumptions under Sections 113A and 113B of the Evidence Act are sometimes treated as if they relieved the prosecution of its foundational burden. This decision restates the correct sequence: the presumptions operate only after the statutory preconditions — dowry-linked cruelty soon before death for Section 304B, cruelty of the statutorily defined kind for Section 498A, instigation or intentional aid for Section 306 — are prima facie shown. For complainants, the lesson is that particulars win cases: dates, demands, witnesses and proximate conduct, not omnibus allegations. For accused persons, the revision jurisdiction under Sections 397/401 CrPC remains a real check on mechanically framed charges, even where a young life has been lost and the instinct to let the trial sort it out is strongest.</p>
</div>
